WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.864
Atomics are valuable, but they are specialized tools.

2
00:00:02.864 --> 00:00:11.099
An atomic object can be accessed concurrently without creating a data race on that object, yet that property does not automatically protect a

3
00:00:11.099 --> 00:00:12.531
higher-level data structure.

4
00:00:12.531 --> 00:00:21.124
If a cache has a map, a size counter, and an eviction list, making one counter atomic does not keep the whole cache consistent.

5
00:00:21.124 --> 00:00:28.643
The design still needs a clear invariant and a synchronization strategy that protects the relationship among all relevant pieces of state.

6
00:00:28.643 --> 00:00:31.865
Lock-free designs raise the review burden even further.

7
00:00:31.865 --> 00:00:36.161
The phrase lock-free does not mean simple, safe, or always faster.

8
00:00:36.161 --> 00:00:40.100
It describes progress properties under certain conditions, not overall design quality.

9
00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:46.544
Memory ordering decisions affect what one thread is allowed to observe from another, and mistakes can be subtle.

10
00:00:46.544 --> 00:00:54.063
Even experienced teams can struggle to reason about these choices without a small scope, strong documentation, targeted tests, and specialized review.

11
00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:59.433
A defensive organization should ask why lock-free behavior is required before accepting the complexity.

12
00:00:59.433 --> 00:01:01.582
Is there a measured contention problem?

13
00:01:01.582 --> 00:01:06.594
Is a blocking lock unacceptable for a specific real-time or low-latency requirement?

14
00:01:06.594 --> 00:01:11.249
Is there a mature library or platform primitive that already solves the problem?

15
00:01:11.249 --> 00:01:18.767
In many systems, a straightforward mutex, sharded lock design, immutable snapshot, or message-passing boundary is safer and easier to maintain.

16
00:01:18.767 --> 00:01:25.570
When atomics or lock-free structures are used, document the invariant, memory ordering assumptions, reclamation strategy, supported operations, and

17
00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:27.360
test evidence in one place.
